==English==
{{was wotd|2018|October|21}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|dum|tatelen}}, {{m|dum|tateren||to babble, chatter}}, originally {{glossary|imitative}}.<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref> The word is cognate with {{cog|nds|tateln}}, {{m|nds|täteln||to cackle, gabble}}.<ref>{{R:Oxford English Dictionary|part of speech=sb|volume=IX|part=2|page=111|column=2}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtæt(ə)l/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtætl̩/|/-ɾl̩/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-tattle.ogg|Audio (AUS)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ætəl|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|tat|tle}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[chatter#Verb|chatter]]; to [[gossip#Verb|gossip]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing Q|act=II|scene=i|page=17|passage=He were an excellent man that were made iuſt in the mid-way between him and Benedick, the one is too like an image and ſaies nothing, and the other too like my ladies eldeſt ſonne, euermore '''tatling'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|chapter=Dedication|page=ix|passage=By this time, My Lord, I doubt not but that you wonder, why I have run off from my Biaſs ſo long together, and made ſo tedious a Digreſſion from Satire to Heroique Poetry. But if You will not excuſe it, by the '''tattling''' Quality of Age, which, as Sir ''{{w|William Davenant}}'' ſays, is always Narrative; yet I hope the uſefulneſs of what I have to ſay on this Subject, will qualifie the remoteneſs of it; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Joseph Glanvil [''i.e.'', {{w|Joseph Glanvill}}]|chapter=The Preface|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lexu86lOepgC&pg=RA1-PP10|title=[[w:Saducismus Triumphatus|Saducismus Triumphatus: Or, Full and Plain Evidence Concerning Witches and Apparitions. In Two Parts.{{nb...|The First Treating of Their Possibility. The Second of Their Real Existence. [...] The Advantages whereof, above the Former, the Reader may Understand out of Dr. H[enry] More’s Account Prefix’d thereunto. With Two Authentick, but Wonderful Stories of Certain Swedish Witches. Done in English by A[nthony] Horneck, D.D.}}]]|edition=3rd|location=London|publisher=Printed for A. L. and sold by Roger Tuckyr,{{nb...|at the Golden Leg, the corner of Salisbury-street, in the Strand.}}|year=1700|oclc=84780159|passage=Nor can any Man be either wiſe or happy till he hath arrived to that greatneſs of ''Mind'', that no more conſiders the '''''tatling''''' of the ''multitude'' than the whiſtling of the ''Wind''.}}
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Alice|volume=I|chapter=VII|pages=297–298|pageref=297|passage=She '''tattled''' on: first to one, then to the other—then to all, till she had '''tattled''' herself out of breath;—and then the orthodox half hour had expired, and the bell was rung, and the carriage ordered, and Mrs. Hare rose to depart.}}
# {{senseid|en|to report others' wrongdoings or violations}}{{lb|en|intransitive|Canada|US|pejorative}} ''Often said of children'': to [[report#Verb|report]] [[incriminating#Adjective|incriminating]] [[information]] about another person, or a person's [[wrongdoing]]; to [[tell on]] somebody. {{defdate|from late 15th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Maryln Appelbaum|chapter=How to Handle Children Who are Disruptive|title=How to Handle Hard-to-handle Preschoolers: A Guide for Early Childhood Educators|location=Thousand Oaks, Calif.|publisher=Corwin Press, {{w|SAGE Publishing}}; Appelbaum Training Institute|year=2009|page=4|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=H1UiUiYJ3NQC&pg=PA4|isbn=978-1-4129-7002-0|passage=There are some children who just like to talk about others. They are not reporting. They are '''tattling''', telling one negative after another. Their goal is to get others in trouble. {{...}} Children sometimes do not mean to '''tattle''' about someone else. They do it because they are having a problem with another child and just don't know any other way to handle the problem.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Renee Gregory|chapter=I’m Gettin’ Broccoli for Dinner|title=Bugs, Bears and S’mores: Songs of the Great Outdoors|location=Pittsburgh, Pa.|publisher=[[w:Dorrance Publishing Company|RoseDog Books]]|year=2009|page=100|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lXD7r7dHrHUC&pg=PA100|isbn=978-1-4349-9530-8|passage=I trapped the girls inside their tent / Someone '''tattled''' on me / Put a frog in the bathroom vent / Someone '''tattled''' on me / Gave my dinner to a bear / Put a snake in Auntie's chair / And a tick in Gramp's rootbeer / Someone '''tattled''' on me}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Deanne A. Crone|author2=Leanne S. Hawken|author3=Robert H. Horner|chapter=Conducting a Functional Behavioral Assessment|title=Building Positive Behavior Support Systems in Schools: Functional Behavioral Assessment|edition=2nd|location=New York, N.Y.; London|publisher=[[w:Guilford Press|The Guilford Press]]|year=2015|section=part II (Embedding Functional Behavioral Assessment within School Systems: Case Examples)|page=39|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=a818BgAAQBAJ&pg=PA39|isbn=978-1-4625-1973-6|passage=Vera is a kindergarten student who loves to be the center of adult attention. She has a quick temper and frequently talks out in class. She also frequently "'''tattles'''" on other students.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[speak]] like a [[baby#Noun|baby]] or [[young#Adjective|young]] [[child]]; to [[babble#Verb|babble]], to [[prattle]]; to speak [[haltingly]]; to [[stutter#Verb|stutter]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|William Caxton}}, transl.|chapter=[[w:Reynard the Fox|The History of Reynard the Fox]]|editor={{w|Henry Morley}}|title=Early Prose Romances:{{nb...|Reynard the Fox, Friar Bacon, Robert the Devil, Guy Warwick, Virgilius, History of Hamlet, Friar Rush}}|series=The Carisbrooke Library|seriesvolume=IV|location=London|publisher=[[w:Routledge|George Routledge and Sons]],{{nb...|Broadway, Ludgate Hill, Glasgow, Manchester, and New York}}|year=1481|year_published=1889|section=chapter XXVII (How Reynart the Fox Came another Time to the Court)|page=108|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/earlyproseromanc00morluoft/page/108|oclc=8103006|passage=But who can give to his [[leasing]] a conclusion, and pronounce it without '''tatelying''', like as it were written tofore him, and that he can so blind the people that his leasing shall better be believed than the truth: that is the man.}}

